@title Science Advances One Funeral at a Time
@edit-time 2026-04-21T03:45Z
The map doesn't get replaced.
The people holding the old map get replaced.
@status proto
@pub-time 2026-03-11T00:00Z
@category knowledge
@term paradigm-shift: TBD
@term history-of-science: TBD
@term change: TBD
@term scientific-revolutions: TBD
@related 002, 009, 011, 015, 045

@argument
A new scientific truth does not triumph by convincing its opponents but by outlasting them — the old map's holders never convert, they retire and die, and the next generation simply grows up inside the new one.

@section(proto)
Thomas Kuhn@footnote(@ref(kuhn, "Kuhn, T.S.
The Structure of Scientific Revolutions.
University of Chicago Press, 1962.")
The foundational work on paradigm shifts — Kuhn showed that normal science operates within a paradigm until anomalies accumulate and trigger revolution, but the old guard rarely converts.) documented carefully that most holders of an old paradigm never come around.
They don't eventually see the light.
@image(019-funerals,Max Planck: science advances one funeral at a time,Wikimedia Commons) They retire and die. The next generation of students, who learned the new map before it had any enemies, who have no old identity to protect, just grow up thinking it's obvious. Max Planck: "A new scientific truth does not triumph by convincing its opponents and making them see the light, but rather because its opponents eventually die and a new generation grows up that is familiar with it."@footnote(@ref(planck, "Planck, M. Scientific Autobiography. Philosophical Library, 1949.") The source of the famous paraphrase "science advances one funeral at a time" — Planck writing from personal experience of the resistance to quantum theory.) This is connected to the magnetization seedpod — the new map spreads not by argument but by generational replacement.

@term Paradigm: Kuhn's term for the framework of assumptions and practices that defines normal science until crisis and revolution.
@term Generational replacement: The mechanism by which new paradigms prevail — not by convincing holdouts but by outlasting them.

